  <h1>India’s Climate Action at COP28 — Strategy and Commitments</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: Dec 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>The 28th Conference of Parties (COP28) to the UNFCCC, held in Dubai in late 2024, marked a critical moment in global climate negotiations. India’s participation was closely watched, as the world’s third-largest emitter balances developmental needs with sustainability obligations. The Indian Express highlighted India’s nuanced strategy: assertive in demanding climate justice, yet pragmatic in pushing renewable energy targets and green finance.
________________________________________
India’s Commitments at COP28
1.	Energy Transition Goals
o	Reiterated target of 500 GW renewable capacity by 2030.
o	Commitment to achieve 50% of energy from non-fossil fuels by 2030.
o	Reaffirmed net-zero by 2070.
2.	Climate Finance
o	India demanded scaled-up financing from developed countries — beyond the $100B annual pledge.
o	Advocated for a new collective quantified goal (NCQG) for post-2025 financing.
3.	Loss and Damage Fund
o	Supported operationalization of the global fund to assist vulnerable nations facing climate disasters.
o	Positioned itself as leader of Global South’s voice in climate justice.
4.	Adaptation and Resilience
o	Highlighted India’s initiatives in climate-resilient agriculture, disaster preparedness, and solar irrigation.
5.	Global Partnerships
o	Promoted International Solar Alliance (ISA) and Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure (CDRI) as global platforms led by India.
________________________________________
India’s Strategy at COP28
•	Equity and CBDR: India emphasized Common But Differentiated Responsibilities, insisting historical polluters must bear larger burden.
•	Balancing Growth: Asserted that energy transition cannot undermine poverty eradication and development.
•	Leadership of Global South: Projected India as a bridge between developed and developing nations.
•	Technological Push: Advocated green hydrogen, EVs, and circular economy as part of sustainable growth.
________________________________________
Challenges for India
•	Coal Dependence: Coal still accounts for ~70% of India’s electricity generation.
•	Financing Gap: IEA estimates India requires $160B annually till 2030 to meet clean energy targets.
•	Implementation Bottlenecks: Land acquisition, transmission infrastructure, and distribution company (DISCOM) inefficiencies.
•	Climate Vulnerability: Increasing frequency of heatwaves, floods, and cyclones.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-III (Environment): Climate change, renewable energy, sustainable development.
•	GS-II (IR): Climate diplomacy, global negotiations.
•	Essay:
o	“Climate justice and development: the twin imperatives for India.”
o	“Balancing ecology and economy in the 21st century.”
•	PSIR Paper II: India’s role in multilateral institutions and leadership in Global South.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Accelerate Renewable Rollout: Expand solar and wind at utility and rooftop levels.<br/>2.	Strengthen Green Finance: Push for sovereign green bonds and incentivize private investment.<br/>3.	Coal Transition Plan: Phased closure of old coal plants while ensuring just transition for workers.<br/>4.	Regional Cooperation: Lead South Asian cooperation on adaptation and disaster management.<br/>5.	Public Awareness: Encourage lifestyle changes under India’s LiFE (Lifestyle for Environment) initiative.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s participation at COP28 showcased a pragmatic balance — asserting climate justice while committing to ambitious green targets. The country positioned itself as a leader of the Global South, pressing for finance and equity while simultaneously scaling its renewable capacity. For UPSC aspirants, India’s COP28 strategy is a case study in combining diplomacy, environmental policy, and developmental priorities.</p>
